H3('Three attempts')
P('The height study contains inconsistencies: the cells are mirrored around the centre, they differ from each other, and the contours are curves, not formulas. It is a reference to measure, nothing else.')
OL(['**Copy it.** The first graph read an image and processed it. That is forbidden, and it is pointless, since I can measure the image myself. The graph was deleted and its files archived.',
    '**Write formulas.** Seven bands of 64 wrangle nodes each, 448 in total, calibrated by a measuring bench until the grey levels matched (the error fell from 1.33 to 0.25). The levels matched and the shapes did not. An averaged cell repeated everywhere loses the petal, and a width-against-height function can only draw a symmetric bottle. This graph, 459 nodes, was deleted too.',
    '**Use real nodes, one band at a time.** Remap, Blend, Function, Fractal Noise, Streak Blur, Turing Patterns, Worley Noise and Ramp, with a measuring loop around them (the first band tests also used Tile Pattern, which the final graph no longer does). That is the graph that stayed.'])
H3('The method that worked')
P('For one band the loop is always the same. Pick one representative cell of the reference, never an average. Measure its contour as a curve, with a mirror when the organ is symmetric. Draw that contour with nodes. Get the volume from the distance to the edge and a profile curve. Get veins and fibres from direction fields. Repeat the cell a whole number of times with a controlled variation per cell. Then compare side by side and with numbers, and only then add the next block of nodes.')
P('The numbers I used: the mean and the spread of the grey levels, the 10th and 90th percentiles, the energy of the edges (which counts fine detail), the correlation of the vertical profile, a structure error (the difference of the two images blurred over three pixels), and for the seams the error between the two border columns against the same error between neighbouring inner columns.')
P('The numbers catch what the eye misses, and the eye catches what the numbers miss. At one point band 0 matched the reference in mean (0.638 against 0.643), in spread (0.160 against 0.157) and in edge energy, and a zoom showed a regular comb of fibres where the reference has a few smooth, curved ones. The statistics were at parity and the picture was wrong. The rule that came out of it: look first, measure second.')
P('**Optimising by ablation.** After a block of nodes, each layer is bypassed in turn and the band is measured again. A layer that changes the band by less than one grey level out of 255, with no effect on the edge energy or on the structure error, is deleted. A layer whose removal lowers the error is harmful and goes as well. The first version of the tool ignored the shadow layers and judged only on the mean, so it kept useless layers and deleted useful ones; it was rewritten. On the leaf, this pass took the band from 157 to 100 nodes at the same quality.')
