H3('The other six bands')
UL(['**Stamens, band 2.** Anthers, filaments and stigma heads are placed from the positions and sizes of 30 elements measured on the reference. The anthers use a Turing pattern, a reaction-diffusion node. It is the heaviest band, 301 nodes, and the place where my graph is still furthest from the reference: the pattern is a maze where the reference has grains.',
    '**Calyx, band 3.** Seven arches and a funnel. At the last measurement the mean tone was 0.470 against 0.469 for the reference.',
    '**Stem, band 4.** My first hairs were regular arcs, because a hair is not a sinusoid: it has a start, a curvature and a length. The hairs are now small stretched seeds blurred along a direction field with Streak Blur (+55 degrees at the top, 0 in the middle, -55 at the bottom). The seam error is 0.0278, under the 0.0296 between neighbouring columns, so it is invisible.',
    '**Leaf, band 5.** Silhouette within 0.01 of the band height, five families of fibres (fine, medium, dark grooves, grain, long fibres) and two outlines: the edge and a second fine arc at about 0.47 of the half width. It took 13 iterations.',
    '**Bud, band 6.** The seam came from different top and bottom values at the two ends of a cell. Making both equal brought the seam score from 100 to 0. A second block of nodes adds the fibrous skin.'])
H3('Seamless by construction')
P('Every pattern repeats a whole number of times across the width, and the network border is set to Wrap, so a tile joins itself exactly. The seam is checked by measurement: the error across the border must fall inside the distribution of the errors between neighbouring inner columns. The final graph has seven band subnets, no wrangle, and the node types you would expect: 696 Blend and 628 Remap nodes carry most of the work, with 63 Fractal Noise, 56 Function, 21 Streak Blur and 5 Turing Patterns.')
H3('Traps worth keeping')
OL(['**Units.** A cell coordinate runs from -1 to +1, so with three cells the centred coordinate in canvas fractions is `u / 6`, not `u / 2`. With `u / 2` every petal came out three times too narrow.',
    '**The Tile Pattern joint.** The node writes a wrong pixel on each joint between tiles, which printed a bright line in the middle of the small petals. The coordinates now come from two Ramp nodes, a repeat ramp for the sawtooth in x and a rotated one in y.',
    '**Moiré.** Sinusoid fibres on a fan that converges fold into aliasing when their spacing falls under two pixels. A fade mask and lower counts fixed it.',
    '**Filler nodes.** Six filler tone curves that squeezed 0.7 into 0.7 flattened a band (the 90th percentile fell from 0.81 to 0.74). A filler must be an identity, and the pruning pass deletes it.',
    '**Resolution.** The resolution parameter of the copnet drives nothing, the export resolution is the one of the image ROP. For a while every export was 1024 and nobody noticed.',
    '**Smoothness.** A mask that goes from 0 to 1 over a short linear ramp leaves a staircase. Masks use a smoothstep at least four pixels wide at the output resolution, and shape curves use monotone cubic interpolation. Applied to every curve, the cubic deformed the tone curves (the mean jumped from 0.64 to 0.76), so it only applies to shapes.',
    '**Save the pictures.** Each iteration needs its own saved image. Early comparison images were overwritten, so the recovered pictures were archived before continuing. The saved height states form the evolution sequence in video 3.'])
H3('4096 and what the pixel size changes')
P('The graph was built at 2048 and exported at 4096. Everything defined in pixels (blur radii, Turing pattern radii, streak lengths) is multiplied by the resolution divided by 2048, through one constant. The 4096 height, brought back to 2048, differs from the 2048 height by 2.4 grey levels out of 255 on average and 27 at the 99th percentile, which comes from the edges getting sharper.')

